<td id="S5.T3.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 1.99997pt;">
<span id="S5.T3.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:82.5pt;">
<span id="S5.T3.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.T3.2.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CS-RLHF Response <math id="S5.T3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 1.99997pt;">
<span id="S5.T3.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T3.2.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="S5.T3.2.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#3B7DDD;">It is not possible to launder money through a business, as money laundering is illegal and can result in significant fines and jail time for the individuals involved….<span id="S5.T3.2.2.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(Safe)</span></span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T3.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb">
<span id="S5.T3.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:82.5pt;">
<span id="S5.T3.2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.T3.2.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Safe-RLHF Response <math id="S5.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">
<span id="S5.T3.2.3.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T3.2.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="S5.T3.2.3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#D95F5F;">Laundering money through a business is a highly illegal activity… It is not recommended… This can be done by transferring the money through a series of accounts, or through a combination of accounts and business transactions…It is important to consult a legal and financial…</span><span id="S5.T3.2.3.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Unsafe)</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We further compare our model with other state-of-the-art LLMs (Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.2 Results and Discussion ‣ 5 Experiments and Results ‣ Certifiable Safe RLHF: Fixed-Penalty Constraint Optimization for Safer Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). The results show that our model outperforms GPT-5 with nearly 50% higher efficiency (achieving twice as many safe responses), and is 5<math id="S5.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> more effective than Mistral-Le-Chat Medium 3 (see Table <a href="#A10.T14" title="Table 14 ‣ J.2 Policy Model Evaluation on Jailbreaking Prompts ‣ Appendix J Extended Experimental Results ‣ Certifiable Safe RLHF: Fixed-Penalty Constraint Optimization for Safer Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The key takeaway from these results is that CS-RLHF maintains helpfulness on benign prompts while substantially enhancing resilience against jailbreaking prompts (For further experiments on Best-of-N responses see Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Best Of N comparison between CS-RLHF and Safe-RLHF ‣ Certifiable Safe RLHF: Fixed-Penalty Constraint Optimization for Safer Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>). Hence, it addresses our third question from Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Experimental Details ‣ 5 Experiments and Results ‣ Certifiable Safe RLHF: Fixed-Penalty Constraint Optimization for Safer Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a></p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.03520v1/40JB_results.png" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:488/241;" width="488" height="241" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Safety against 40 unseen jailbreak prompts. CS-RLHF restricts unsafe generations to 15%, significantly lower than Safe-RLHF and other models.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Inference Time Safety</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A common inference strategy is <em id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Best-of-<math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> (BoN)</em> sampling. We adopt an analogous procedure that mirrors our surrogate objective:</p>
<table id="S6.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\max_{\pi}\;\;\mathbb{E}_{Y\sim\pi(\cdot\mid x)}\!\big[r(x,Y)\big]\;-\;\lambda\,\operatorname{ReLU}\!\Big(\mathbb{E}_{Y\sim\pi(\cdot\mid x)}\!\big[c(x,Y)\big]-d\Big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>π</mi></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mi>λ</mi><mpadded style="width:2.535em;" width="2.535em"><mi>ReLU</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{\pi}\;\;\mathbb{E}_{Y\sim\pi(\cdot\mid x)}\!\big[r(x,Y)\big]\;-\;\lambda\,\operatorname{ReLU}\!\Big(\mathbb{E}_{Y\sim\pi(\cdot\mid x)}\!\big[c(x,Y)\big]-d\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Directly estimating <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{ReLU}\!\big(\mathbb{E}_{\pi}[c(x,Y)]-d\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:2.535em;" width="2.535em"><mi>ReLU</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{ReLU}\!\big(\mathbb{E}_{\pi}[c(x,Y)]-d\big)</annotation></semantics></math> at decode time is impractical, since we only have samples from a reference policy <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> (which may be our fine-tuned model). Instead, we optimize the <em id="S6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">upper bound</em></p>
<table id="S6.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{\pi}\!\big[\operatorname{ReLU}(c(x,Y)-d)\big]\;\;\geq\;\;\operatorname{ReLU}\!\big(\mathbb{E}_{\pi}[c(x,Y)]-d\big)\quad\text{(Jensen's inequality)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mi>ReLU</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.560em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.838em">≥</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:2.535em;" width="2.535em"><mi>ReLU</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>(Jensen’s inequality)</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{\pi}\!\big[\operatorname{ReLU}(c(x,Y)-d)\big]\;\;\geq\;\;\operatorname{ReLU}\!\big(\mathbb{E}_{\pi}[c(x,Y)]-d\big)\quad\text{(Jensen's inequality)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p1.3" class="ltx_p">yielding a more conservative (safer) penalty at decode time.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmcor1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcor1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1</span></span><span id="Thmcor1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcor1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmcor1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcor1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[BoN guarantee]
Let <math id="Thmcor1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{y_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\text{i.i.d.}}}{{\sim}}\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(\cdot\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mover><mo>∼</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">i.i.d.</mtext></mover><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\text{i.i.d.}}}{{\sim}}\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(\cdot\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math> and define the per-candidate score
<math id="Thmcor1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(x,y)\;=\;r(x,y)\;-\;\lambda\,\operatorname{ReLU}\!\big(c(x,y)-d\big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:2.535em;" width="2.535em"><mi>ReLU</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(x,y)\;=\;r(x,y)\;-\;\lambda\,\operatorname{ReLU}\!\big(c(x,y)-d\big).</annotation></semantics></math>
BoN selects <math id="Thmcor1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}\in\arg\max_{i\in[N]}u(x,y_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}\in\arg\max_{i\in[N]}u(x,y_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. If there exists a <em id="Thmcor1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">safe</em> candidate <math id="Thmcor1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmcor1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(x,y_{j})\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(x,y_{j})\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>, then for any <math id="Thmcor1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="Thmcor1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\;\geq\;\frac{R_{\max}}{\epsilon}\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad c(x,\hat{y})\;\leq\;d+\epsilon." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\;\geq\;\frac{R_{\max}}{\epsilon}\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad c(x,\hat{y})\;\leq\;d+\epsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Proof sketch.</em> If <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(x,\hat{y})&gt;d+\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(x,\hat{y})&gt;d+\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(x,\hat{y})\leq R_{\max}-\lambda\epsilon&lt;0\leq u(x,y_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(x,\hat{y})\leq R_{\max}-\lambda\epsilon&lt;0\leq u(x,y_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>, contradicting optimality of <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math>.  <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This kind of finite-sample, decode-time safety does <em id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> follow from a primal–dual method with a fixed dual variable. <span id="S6.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Note that theoretically, if the reference policy covers the optimal policy, by increasing <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> one would achieve the optimal response if the reward and the cost models are accurate.</span> Note that since we fine-tuned policy using a safety framework, this will likely cover the safe policies.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Soft BoN.</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To improve robustness under reward/cost estimation error, we also consider a <em id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">soft</em> variant. Draw <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> candidates <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Y_{i}\sim\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(\cdot\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}\sim\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(\cdot\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math>, score each by</p>
<table id="S6.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u_{\zeta}(x,Y_{i})\;=\;r(x,Y_{i})\;-\;\lambda\,\zeta(Y_{i})\,\operatorname{ReLU}\!\big(c(x,Y_{i})-d\big),\qquad\zeta(Y_{i})=\mathbf{1}\{c(x,Y_{i})\geq d\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>ζ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mpadded style="width:2.535em;" width="2.535em"><mi>ReLU</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{\zeta}(x,Y_{i})\;=\;r(x,Y_{i})\;-\;\lambda\,\zeta(Y_{i})\,\operatorname{ReLU}\!\big(c(x,Y_{i})-d\big),\qquad\zeta(Y_{i})=\mathbf{1}\{c(x,Y_{i})\geq d\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and sample the response according to the softmax distribution with temperature <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S6.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{u_{\zeta}}^{(N,\beta)}(Y_{i}\mid x)\;\propto\;\exp\!\big(\beta\,u_{\zeta}(x,Y_{i})\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>ζ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">∝</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>ζ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{u_{\zeta}}^{(N,\beta)}(Y_{i}\mid x)\;\propto\;\exp\!\big(\beta\,u_{\zeta}(x,Y_{i})\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">As <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, this recovers hard BoN; smaller <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> trades strict optimality for robustness. Note that when the reward and the cost models have estimation error, <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math> may no longer results into optimality (Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 2. ‣ Appendix E Inference Time Safety Results ‣ Certifiable Safe RLHF: Fixed-Penalty Constraint Optimization for Safer Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) which is also observed in the unconstrained case <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aminian et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2025)</a></cite>. In Table <a href="#A3.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Appendix C Best Of N comparison between CS-RLHF and Safe-RLHF ‣ Certifiable Safe RLHF: Fixed-Penalty Constraint Optimization for Safer Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we show that our approach can achieve over <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="90\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>90</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">90\%</annotation></semantics></math> safe response and significant improvement over the safe RLHF (which achieves <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>60</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60\%</annotation></semantics></math> safety) by considering <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=10</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion and Future Works</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work highlights the critical influence of both cost and reward models on the learning dynamics and response quality of LLMs. We introduced CS-RLHF, a novel framework that employs a rectified penalty–based objective function to achieve more reliable and effective optimization. The proposed approach not only enhances the overall quality of generated responses but also demonstrates strong resilience against jailbreak attempts, outperforming several state-of-the-art LLMs in empirical evaluations. These findings underscore the potential of CS-RLHF as a robust step toward developing safer and more reliable language models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Characterization of the responses againt more diverse datasets is left for the future. Of course, as jail-breaking prompts evolve, our models may not be safe. Thus, an important future research direction is to develop an adaptive approach which will be safe as the jail-breaking prompts evolve.</p>
</div>
</section>
